WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.680
We'll now talk about the algorithmic
problem of sorting by reversals.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.120
Let's start from a short chromosome
consisting of just 10 blocks

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.260
and we'll number them in the order
1, 2, 3 to 10.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:21.410
Now a reversal... we can think about a
reversal as something that breaks

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:25.830
a chromosome at two places and glues the
resulting pieces in

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:31.010
a new order as shown on this slide.
And as a result, the

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:36.170
reversal introduces two breakpoints,
disruptions in the gene order.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:40.720
For example, +3 and -8 are not
supposed to be together but they are

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:50.386
together in the new permutation, and the
same thing applies to -4 and +9.

00:00:50.386 --> 00:00:54.820
So let's take a look at one of the possible
rearrangement scenarios.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:01:00.560
In this case, transforming a permutation
into an identity permutation which is

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.710
+1 +2 +8 with these five reversals.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:09.438
Is it the shortest scenario?
No, because there is a shorter scenario

00:01:09.438 --> 00:01:14.630
that transforms the same permutation into
identity with just four reversals.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.190
And we will define reversal distance as
the minimum

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:23.715
number of reversals to transform one
permutation into another.

00:01:23.715 --> 00:01:29.170
Biologists are often interested in
shortest scenarios

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:33.880
for transforming one genome into another
because

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.600
this shorter scenario often corresponds to
the biological

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.000
scenario or comes close to the biological
scenarios.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.210
So we now have a transformation of one

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.760
genome into another with just four
reversals,

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.950
but maybe there is even faster
transformations

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.570
of these genomes with just three
reversals.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.900
And the reversal distance problem is the

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.660
problem of calculating the reversal
distance

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.860
between two permutations.
Input: two permutations,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.330
Output: the reversal distance between them.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:11.742
We will also be talking about sorting by
reversal problem

00:02:11.742 --> 00:02:16.870
which uses an input of just a
single permutation

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:21.190
and ask for the reversal distance between
this permutation

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:25.520
and the identity permutation +1, +2, ..., +n

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.860
Why identity permutations?

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:32.465
Because we are at liberty to label the
final permutation the way we

00:02:32.465 --> 00:02:37.760
want and we usually label this as
+1, +2, +3, ....

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:45.430
Now, what would be the most natural
algorithm for sorting by reversals?

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.980
I'm not saying good algorithm, but the
most natural one?

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:53.080
Let's look at this mouse permutation, for
an example of this case.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.990
+1 is already in the right position in
this permutation,

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:02.860
but +2 is not.
However, we can use a

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:08.710
reversal, shown in bold here, to bring
plus 2 at the right position.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.010
That's what we've done right here.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.260
We brought +2 at the right position,
but now it has a wrong sign.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:16.020
Not a problem.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.190
We will reverse the sign using a short
reversal right here.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.200
Now, we placed both

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.460
+1 and +2at the right position,
but -3 is at the wrong position.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:32.400
Not a problem, we'll find a reversal that
brings it at the right position.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.040
Now let's take care of +4.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.980
Once again, let's find a reversal that
brings it to the right position.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:41.720
It now has wrong sign, but we will reverse it.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:48.032
And we will continue doing this until we
finally, using this Greedy algorithm,

00:03:48.032 --> 00:03:54.255
transform mouse into Philip.
And after we looked at the Greedy algorithm

00:03:54.255 --> 00:03:58.622
for sorting by reversal, you may
notice that of course, it's not optimal.

00:03:58.622 --> 00:04:05.390
We used 11 reversals to transform mouse
into human permutations, but we know

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:11.040
from the first slide in this lecture that
it can be done in just seven reversals.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.620
So, maybe we should come up with

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.920
a better algorithm, and to do this we

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.250
will have to define the notion of
a breakpoint graph.

